{
  "subtopics": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "label": "Difficulty of measuring atomic radius",
      "coverage": "explained",
      "evidence": "An atom has a nucleus in the center, surrounded by a \"cloud\" of electrons. Because this cloud doesn't have a sharp boundary, **you can't just take a ruler and measure the radius from the center to the edge.**"
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "label": "Covalent bond formation and measurement",
      "coverage": "explained",
      "evidence": "When two identical atoms form a covalent bond, their electron clouds overlap, and their nuclei are locked at a stable, measurable distance from each other. \n\nScientists can easily measure the distance between the centers (the nuclei) of these two bonded atoms using X-ray technology."
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "label": "Definition and formula of covalent radius",
      "coverage": "explained",
      "evidence": "The covalent radius is simply half the distance between the nuclei of two identical atoms joined by a single covalent bond."
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "label": "Periodic trend down a group",
      "coverage": "explained",
      "evidence": "Every time you move down a row, you are adding a completely new **energy level (shell)** of electrons. It’s like putting on another bulky winter jacket. The outer electrons are further from the center, so the covalent radius increases."
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "label": "Periodic trend across a period",
      "coverage": "explained",
      "evidence": "As you go left to right, you are adding more protons to the nucleus and more electrons to the *same* energy level. \n* More protons mean a stronger positive charge in the center. The nucleus acts like a stronger magnet, pulling that electron cloud closer and tighter toward the middle. This shrinks the atom, so the covalent radius decreases."
    }
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "id": "e1",
      "label": "Fluffy cloud or cotton ball analogy",
      "kind": "analogy",
      "subtopic_ids": ["s1"],
      "evidence": "Imagine trying to measure the exact size of a fluffy cloud or a cotton ball. It’s hard, right? There is no hard outer surface—just a fuzzy edge."
    },
    {
      "id": "e2",
      "label": "Calculating the covalent radius of chlorine",
      "kind": "worked_example",
      "subtopic_ids": ["s3"],
      "evidence": "In a chlorine molecule ($\\text{Cl}_2$), the distance between the two chlorine nuclei is about **198 picometers** (pm). \n* To find the covalent radius of a single chlorine atom, just divide that by 2:\n  $$\\frac{198}{2} = 99\\text{ pm}$$\n* So, the covalent radius of chlorine is **99 pm**."
    },
